T05.01|Revenue describes consideration earned by transferring promised goods or services, rather than every receipt into a bank account. Begin with the customer contract: identify the promises, determine consideration, allocate it to distinct obligations, and recognise it as those obligations are satisfied. The applicable framework determines the exact recognition criteria.~~A cash sale can create revenue and cash together. A credit sale creates revenue and a receivable first. An advance normally creates cash and a contract liability until performance occurs. This timing distinction explains why strong sales can coexist with weak cash collection. Other operating income should be examined by its nature; the label alone does not establish that it is comparable to core product sales.|Point-in-time revenue: Recognition occurs when the relevant control-transfer conditions are met, such as qualifying delivery of a product.;;Over-time revenue: Recognition follows performance over time when the applicable criteria are met, using an appropriate progress measure.;;Recurring and non-recurring sales: Subscriptions and repeat purchases may be more forecastable than isolated contracts; recurrence does not override recognition rules.|An illustrative company receives ₹12 lakh on 1 April for twelve months of evenly delivered service. Assume the service qualifies for over-time recognition and there are no taxes or refunds.~~Record ₹12 cash and ₹12 contract liability on receipt.;;Recognise ₹1 revenue for each month of service delivered.;;After three months, revenue totals ₹3 and the remaining contract liability is ₹9.~~Cash received is ₹12, but revenue earned to date is ₹3; the liability represents the remaining service obligation.~~If the company spent the entire advance immediately, it would still owe nine months of service. Cash availability and completed performance therefore answer different questions.|Read the revenue-recognition policy and contract terms.;;Separate price, volume, mix, timing and collectability as drivers.;;Reconcile revenue to receivables, contract assets, advances and customer cash collections.
T05.02|Gross versus net presentation asks whether the entity supplies the promised good or service itself or arranges for another party to supply it. A principal generally recognises the consideration for its own promise gross. An agent generally recognises the fee or commission earned for arranging supply. Under IFRS 15, control before transfer is central; indicators support the assessment rather than mechanically replacing it.~~Transaction volume, gross merchandise value and revenue are therefore different measures. A platform can facilitate ₹100 crore of purchases while earning only a small fee. Comparing its revenue margin with a retailer's margin without adjusting the business model can produce a misleading conclusion.|Principal: Controls the specified good or service before it reaches the customer; gross presentation includes the related cost of supply.;;Agent: Arranges supply by another party and generally recognises its net fee.;;Mixed arrangements: An entity may act as principal for one distinct promise and agent for another within the same broader transaction.|A marketplace arranges ₹100 lakh of customer purchases and receives an 8% fee. A retailer buys the same goods for ₹92 lakh and resells them for ₹100 lakh. Assume the marketplace is an agent and the retailer is a principal.~~Marketplace revenue = ₹100 × 8% = ₹8; it does not record ₹92 as its own cost of goods.;;Retailer revenue = ₹100 and cost of goods = ₹92.;;Both generate ₹8 before their additional operating costs, despite very different reported revenue.~~Gross merchandise value is ₹100 for the marketplace, whereas its recognised revenue is ₹8.~~The apparent 100% marketplace gross margin does not prove superior economics: customer acquisition, technology, fulfilment and support costs still matter.|Identify the specified promise and control before transfer.;;Read gross-versus-net policies and changes.;;Compare contribution and cash economics alongside revenue-based ratios.
T05.03|The invoice's headline price is not always the amount of revenue the company earns. Discounts, rebates, expected returns and other variable consideration can reduce the amount retained. Taxes collected for a government are generally amounts collected on behalf of another party, subject to the relevant rules.~~An analyst should distinguish a price reduction from a selling expense and identify whether return estimates reflect current evidence. A subsequent return can reverse recognised sales and affect receivables, refund obligations, inventory recovery and cost of sales. Revenue growth funded by aggressive rebates may therefore conceal weaker realised pricing.|Upfront discount: Reduces the agreed selling price immediately.;;Volume rebate: Changes effective consideration when contractual purchase thresholds or other conditions are met.;;Sales return: Reverses eligible consideration and requires assessment of refund obligations and recoverable goods.;;Indirect tax collected: An amount payable to the government rather than automatically the seller's income.|Assume a ₹100 lakh list-price sale, a ₹5 lakh contractual discount, estimated eligible refunds of ₹3 lakh, and ₹18 lakh separately collected illustrative tax. Ignore recovery costs.~~Start with ₹100 list consideration.;;Subtract ₹5 discount and ₹3 expected refunds to obtain ₹92 retained consideration under the simplified assumptions.;;Keep the ₹18 tax collection separate as an obligation to the government.~~Recognised revenue in the simplified case is ₹92; gross invoice receipts and revenue need not match.~~Revising the return estimate changes current reporting even before refunds are paid. The accounting must also consider whether any inventory is recoverable.|Reconcile gross billings to recognised net sales.;;Review rebate contracts, return experience and estimation changes.;;Compare net realised price with customer acquisition and promotional spending.
T05.04|Cost of goods sold matches the carrying cost of inventory sold against the related revenue. It is not necessarily equal to cash paid to suppliers or goods purchased during the year. Unsold qualifying goods remain inventory, while sold goods move into expense. Service businesses similarly recognise relevant service-delivery costs, though their account structure differs.~~For a simple trading business, cost of goods sold equals opening inventory plus net purchases minus closing inventory. Manufacturing adds production-cost flows. Analyse freight, direct labour, subcontractors and production overhead consistently before comparing margins; companies can classify similar costs differently.|Trading cost of goods sold: The inventory cost of merchandise purchased and resold.;;Manufacturing cost of sales: The cost of completed goods sold, incorporating relevant materials, labour and production overhead.;;Cost of services: Costs attributable to delivering services, such as delivery personnel or third-party service inputs.;;Period expenses: Costs that are not included in inventory under the relevant recognition rules.|Opening inventory is ₹20 lakh, net purchases are ₹80 lakh and closing inventory is ₹25 lakh. Revenue is ₹120 lakh. Assume no write-downs or other inventory adjustments.~~Goods available for sale = ₹20 + ₹80 = ₹100.;;Cost of goods sold = ₹100 − ₹25 = ₹75.;;Gross profit = ₹120 − ₹75 = ₹45; gross margin = ₹45/₹120 = 37.5%.~~Purchases of ₹80 differ from expense of ₹75 because inventory increased by ₹5.~~Supplier payments can differ again because trade payables change. Inventory, expense and cash payment must each be traced separately.|Reconcile opening inventory, purchases, production and closing inventory.;;Check inventory valuation and expense classification.;;Separate changes in unit input cost, selling price and product mix.
T05.05|A factory transforms raw materials into work in progress and then finished goods. Qualifying production costs are accumulated in these assets until sale, rather than being expensed simply because cash was spent. Direct materials and labour can be traced to production; production overhead requires systematic allocation.~~Under IAS 2, fixed production overhead allocation reflects normal capacity, and abnormal waste is not simply hidden in inventory. Producing more units can temporarily shift costs from the income statement to the balance sheet, but it cannot turn unwanted stock into cash. Examine inventory growth, utilisation and write-down risk together.|Raw materials: Inputs available for production.;;Work in progress: Incomplete products containing accumulated production costs.;;Finished goods: Completed products awaiting sale.;;Abnormal waste and unallocated overhead: Costs that may require current expense rather than indefinite storage in inventory.|A factory begins with no inventory, incurs ₹30 lakh of eligible production costs, and completes 1,000 identical units. It sells 600 units for ₹30 lakh in total. Assume equal unit cost and no waste.~~Unit production cost = ₹30 lakh/1,000 = ₹3,000.;;Expense for 600 units sold = 600 × ₹3,000 = ₹18 lakh.;;Closing finished-goods inventory = 400 × ₹3,000 = ₹12 lakh.~~Gross profit is ₹30 − ₹18 = ₹12 lakh; the remaining ₹12 lakh of production cost is still an asset.~~Producing the 400 unsold units consumed resources even though their cost has not yet reached profit. Future demand and recoverability determine whether that asset remains useful.|Track quantities and costs separately.;;Compare production with sales and normal capacity.;;Investigate ageing, abnormal losses and net realisable value.
T05.06|Gross profit is the amount left after recognised revenue pays for recognised cost of sales. Gross margin divides that amount by revenue and expresses how much of each sales rupee remains before the operating expenses outside cost of sales. It is not net profit, cash flow or return on capital.~~Margin and markup use different denominators. Margin measures profit relative to the selling price; markup measures profit relative to cost. A higher gross margin can result from price increases, input savings, richer mix or changed accounting classification. Identify which cause explains the movement before treating it as sustainable.|Gross profit: Revenue minus cost of sales, expressed as money.;;Gross margin: Gross profit divided by revenue.;;Markup: Gross profit divided by cost; it differs mathematically from margin.;;Contribution margin: Revenue minus variable costs, which need not have the same perimeter as accounting cost of sales.|A product costs ₹80 and sells for ₹100. The seller then offers a 10% price discount without changing unit cost.~~Original profit = ₹100 − ₹80 = ₹20; margin = 20%; markup = 25%.;;Discounted price = ₹90 and gross profit = ₹10.;;Discounted margin = ₹10/₹90 = 11.11%.~~A 10% price cut halves gross profit per unit, even though the selling price falls only one tenth.~~Discounts can have a much larger percentage effect on profit than on revenue. The firm would need twice the units to preserve gross profit, assuming capacity and all other costs permit it.|State the denominator and cost perimeter.;;Bridge price, input costs and product mix.;;Test whether volume gains compensate for lower profit per unit.
T05.07|Employee expense reflects compensation earned through employee service, including more than salary paid this month. Bonuses, payroll-related contributions, leave obligations, retirement benefits and share-based awards can introduce estimates and different payment dates. Some eligible employee costs may form part of an asset's production or development cost rather than an immediate period expense.~~The expense-to-cash difference creates accruals or prepayments. Employee cost analysis should separate headcount, average compensation, location mix, utilisation and incentive design. A decline in cash payroll is not proof that recognised labour cost has fallen, especially where bonuses or benefits remain unpaid.|Short-term benefits: Wages, salaries and qualifying short-term bonuses or leave benefits.;;Defined-contribution benefits: Contributions owed to a separate plan under the arrangement's terms.;;Defined-benefit obligations: Promised benefits requiring actuarial measurement and risk assessment.;;Share-based compensation: Awards measured and recognised under the applicable award-accounting rules.|An illustrative firm recognises ₹10 lakh of salary service, ₹2 lakh of earned bonuses and ₹1 lakh of employer plan contributions. It pays ₹9 lakh salary and ₹1 lakh contributions; bonuses remain unpaid.~~Total current expense = ₹10 + ₹2 + ₹1 = ₹13, assuming none is capitalised.;;Cash payments = ₹9 + ₹1 = ₹10.;;Unpaid salary ₹1 plus bonuses ₹2 create ₹3 of related liabilities.~~Expense is ₹13 while cash outflow is ₹10.~~When the ₹3 is paid later, the existing liability falls; the same earned compensation is not expensed again solely because payment occurs.|Separate compensation categories and recognition rules.;;Reconcile expense with cash, accruals and capitalised costs.;;Assess recurring compensation and contingent future commitments.
T05.08|Selling, general and administrative expenses support customer acquisition, corporate administration and business operations outside the cost-of-sales perimeter. Examples include sales teams, advertising, headquarters, finance and legal support. The classification depends on the business and reporting presentation, so a ratio is meaningful only after understanding what it contains.~~These costs can be fixed, variable or step-fixed. Reducing them can improve current earnings but may also weaken sales, service or controls. Assess whether claimed savings remove unnecessary expenditure, shift costs elsewhere, or merely defer essential spending.|Selling expenses: Costs of marketing, selling and distribution outside the chosen cost-of-sales definition.;;General and administrative expenses: Shared management and support functions.;;Fixed and step-fixed costs: Costs that remain stable within a capacity range but increase when support capacity expands.;;Variable selling costs: Costs such as commissions that change with relevant activity.|Assume gross profit ₹50 lakh and SG&A of ₹20 lakh, comprising ₹12 fixed administration and ₹8 sales commissions at the current volume. Revenue grows 25% with unchanged gross margin and commission rate.~~Gross profit becomes ₹50 × 1.25 = ₹62.5.;;Commissions become ₹8 × 1.25 = ₹10, while assumed fixed administration stays ₹12.;;Profit before other charges becomes ₹62.5 − ₹22 = ₹40.5 versus ₹30 originally.~~The increase is ₹10.5 because fixed costs do not grow proportionately within the assumed capacity range.~~This operating leverage disappears if the firm needs an additional support team or if growth requires unusually high advertising spending.|Define which costs sit inside SG&A.;;Classify activity drivers and capacity thresholds.;;Evaluate savings against customer outcomes, future growth and control quality.
T05.09|Research seeks new knowledge, while development applies knowledge to a plan or design before commercial production or use. These phases can have different accounting treatment. Under IFRS, research expenditure is expensed, and development expenditure is capitalised only when all applicable recognition criteria are demonstrated; expected success alone is insufficient. US GAAP treatment can differ.~~Capitalisation moves qualifying cost into an asset and subsequent amortisation; immediate expensing charges current profit. The economic cash spent can be identical under both treatments. Comparing innovative businesses therefore requires understanding policies, project eligibility, useful lives and impairment, rather than equating reported earnings with innovation productivity.|Research: Investigation before the qualifying development stage, generally expensed under IAS 38.;;Qualifying development: Costs meeting the relevant technical, commercial, resource and measurement criteria.;;Amortisation: Allocation of a recognised finite-lived development asset over its useful life.;;Impairment: Reduction when the asset's recoverable amount no longer supports its carrying amount.|A hypothetical project spends ₹6 lakh on research and ₹9 lakh on later development. Assume only the latter qualifies for capitalisation under the applicable framework and ignore tax.~~Expense ₹6 research in the period.;;Recognise ₹9 qualifying development as an asset.;;If it becomes available for use next year with a three-year life and no residual value, annual straight-line amortisation is ₹3.~~Total initial cash spending is ₹15, but the simplified initial expense is ₹6.~~Later project failure may trigger impairment. Capitalisation changes profit timing and the asset balance; it does not recover the money spent.|Identify the reporting framework and phase of each project.;;Review evidence supporting recognition and useful life.;;Compare cash R&D, capitalised additions, amortisation and impairment.
T05.10|Depreciation and amortisation allocate the depreciable amount of assets over periods benefiting from their use. Depreciation concerns tangible assets; amortisation concerns finite-lived intangibles. Impairment is a separate assessment of whether the carrying amount is recoverable. None of these entries is itself a new cash payment.~~These expenses connect investing decisions to subsequent profit. Adding them back in a cash-flow reconciliation reverses the non-cash charge; it does not make the original investment free. Future replacement spending can remain substantial even where EBITDA looks strong.|Straight-line allocation: An equal charge over the assumed useful life.;;Units-of-production allocation: A charge linked to actual use or output when appropriate.;;Accelerated allocation: Greater expense in earlier periods where the consumption pattern supports it.;;Impairment: A recoverability adjustment rather than a routine scheduled allocation.|An illustrative machine costs ₹24 lakh, has a ₹4 lakh residual value and a five-year useful life. After two years, assume its recoverable amount is ₹13 lakh.~~Depreciable amount = ₹24 − ₹4 = ₹20.;;Annual straight-line depreciation = ₹20/5 = ₹4; carrying amount after two years is ₹16.;;Impairment under the simplified recoverability assumption = ₹16 − ₹13 = ₹3.~~The year-two carrying amount becomes ₹13 after the impairment; the impairment itself does not pay ₹3 cash.~~Subsequent depreciation must reflect the revised carrying amount, remaining life and residual-value assumptions. Changing estimates affects future allocation and must be justified.|Reconcile additions, disposals, depreciation and impairment.;;Review useful lives, residual values and utilisation.;;Relate non-cash expense to actual maintenance and expansion capex.
T05.11|EBIT includes operating expenses such as depreciation and amortisation before financing and income tax, subject to the measure's stated definition. EBITDA adds back depreciation and amortisation. Company-defined operating profit and adjusted EBITDA can differ in perimeter, particularly for investment income or exceptional items. Reconcile labels instead of assuming equivalence.~~EBITDA can help compare operating earnings before some investment and financing effects, but omits capital spending, working-capital funding, cash taxes and debt service. EBIT better reflects an allocation of past asset cost, yet still differs from cash available today. Both require context about recurring exclusions and the business model.|EBIT: Earnings before interest and tax under a stated definition.;;EBITDA: EBIT plus depreciation and amortisation under a consistent perimeter.;;Adjusted EBITDA: A management measure with additional exclusions that require scrutiny.;;Operating cash flow: Cash from operating activities, affected by accruals and applicable cash-flow classification.|Revenue is ₹120 lakh, operating costs excluding depreciation are ₹80, depreciation is ₹10, working-capital investment is ₹15 and assumed operating cash tax is ₹6. Ignore all other adjustments.~~EBITDA = ₹120 − ₹80 = ₹40.;;EBIT = ₹40 − ₹10 = ₹30.;;Illustrative operating cash after working capital and tax = ₹40 − ₹15 − ₹6 = ₹19, before any other classifications or adjustments.~~EBITDA ₹40 is considerably higher than this illustrative cash measure ₹19.~~If the firm also needs replacement equipment, free cash flow falls further. A valuation based on EBITDA still needs to account for reinvestment and non-equity claims.|Reconcile reported subtotals and adjustments.;;Identify recurring costs excluded from management measures.;;Trace earnings into taxes, working capital, capex and financing needs.
T05.12|Interest is the cost of using borrowed funds over time; principal repayment returns the borrowed amount. Interest expense can include effective-interest adjustments or other financing costs, while borrowing-cost capitalisation may apply to qualifying assets under the relevant framework. Interest income arises from financial assets and should be assessed separately from a non-financial company's core operating sales.~~Cash interest and recognised interest need not match because fees, discounts, accruals and effective-rate measurement affect timing. Debt affordability requires cash-flow and maturity analysis, not just a comparison of reported interest with profit.|Interest expense: Recognised financing cost for the period.;;Capitalised borrowing costs: Qualifying financing costs included in an asset rather than immediately expensed.;;Interest income: Income earned on financial assets under the relevant measurement rules.;;Principal repayment: Reduction in the debt obligation, ordinarily not an income-statement expense.|Assume opening debt ₹100 lakh, annual interest 8%, ₹20 principal repaid at year-end and no fees or capitalisation. Operating EBIT is ₹25.~~Interest for the year = ₹100 × 8% = ₹8.;;Pre-tax profit = ₹25 − ₹8 = ₹17.;;Debt closing balance = ₹100 − ₹20 = ₹80; total debt-related cash paid is ₹28.~~The ₹20 principal reduces debt and cash, whereas the ₹8 interest reduces profit under these assumptions.~~The company may report ₹17 profit before tax while owing ₹28 in debt-related cash payments. Refinancing and cash generation therefore matter alongside profitability.|Read borrowing terms, fee accounting and interest accruals.;;Separate interest expense, capitalised amounts and cash paid.;;Stress rates, maturity concentration and refinancing access.
T05.13|Other income is a presentation label that can combine economically different items: deposit interest, dividends, investment fair-value changes, disposal gains and miscellaneous receipts. Separate them by source, recurrence and cash consequence. An operating-business forecast should not automatically repeat a gain merely because it appears in the latest year's profit.~~Disposal proceeds and disposal profit differ. Profit measures sale consideration against the disposed asset's carrying amount, while cash flow reports the actual eligible cash receipt. Fair-value gains can raise profit without a sale or cash inflow, depending on the instrument's measurement classification.|Realised disposal gain: Consideration exceeds the asset's carrying amount on disposal.;;Unrealised measurement gain: A change in recognised value without sale, with profit or OCI treatment depending on the rules.;;Interest and dividend income: Returns on financial assets, requiring cash-timing and recurrence analysis.;;Non-recurring miscellaneous income: Items whose origin and repeatability must be investigated.|Equipment with a carrying amount of ₹8 lakh is sold for ₹11 lakh cash. Assume no transaction costs or taxes.~~Remove the ₹8 carrying amount from equipment.;;Record the ₹11 cash receipt.;;Recognise a ₹3 disposal gain; when reconciling indirect operating cash flow, remove that gain if the related cash belongs in investing.~~Profit increases ₹3, while investing cash inflow is ₹11 under the assumed classification.~~Counting both the gain inside operating cash flow and the full sale proceeds inside investing cash flow would overstate total cash movement.|Break down other income using notes and transaction evidence.;;Distinguish recurring returns from gains and remeasurements.;;Keep profit recognition and cash-flow classification consistent.
T05.14|An exceptional label signals management's claim that an item should be viewed separately; it does not prove that the expense is economically irrelevant. Normalisation asks what earnings would represent sustainable operations under a stated purpose. A cost can be unusual in amount but recurring in business necessity.~~Restructuring, litigation, impairment and acquisition costs require case-specific treatment. Even a legitimate exclusion from an operating comparison may represent real cash paid and a value consequence. Keep a bridge from reported profit to every adjusted measure, then assess whether forecasting or valuation still includes the underlying obligation.|Non-recurring event: A genuinely unusual event that may warrant separate forecasting treatment.;;Recurring adjustment: An exclusion that appears repeatedly and may be part of normal economics.;;Non-cash charge: An item without immediate cash payment that can still reflect a real loss of value.;;Cash restructuring cost: A current or future outflow that does not disappear because adjusted earnings exclude it.|A firm reports EBIT ₹20 lakh and adds back ₹6 restructuring to present adjusted EBIT ₹26. The same type of programme cost ₹5 last year and is forecast to cost ₹4 next year.~~Reconcile ₹20 + ₹6 = ₹26.;;Investigate whether restructuring is a continuing requirement rather than a completed isolated event.;;For an illustrative sustainable forecast, include the expected ₹4 future cost rather than simply adopting ₹26 as recurring earnings.~~Adjusted EBIT is a useful disclosed comparison only when its exclusions and forecasting implications are explicit.~~The analyst need not accept or reject every adjustment together. A fair assessment tests each exclusion and includes real expected cash consequences.|Build the reported-to-adjusted bridge.;;Review the same exclusions across multiple years.;;Separate comparison adjustments from forecast cash obligations.
T05.15|Profit before tax measures accounting performance before income-tax expense. Tax expense may comprise current tax on taxable income and deferred tax arising from qualifying differences between accounting and tax recognition. Tax paid is a cash measure affected by instalments, withholding, settlements and timing. These three numbers can legitimately differ.~~The effective tax rate equals income-tax expense divided by pre-tax profit, when that denominator is meaningful. It can vary due to jurisdictions, permanent differences, losses, incentives and deferred-tax changes. A current-year unusual rate is not automatically the appropriate long-run valuation tax assumption.|Current tax expense: Tax associated with taxable income for the relevant period under applicable law.;;Deferred tax expense or benefit: Recognition of qualifying future tax consequences of temporary differences and other permitted items.;;Effective tax rate: Accounting tax expense relative to pre-tax profit.;;Marginal cash tax assumption: Tax on an additional relevant cash-flow amount under specified rules.|Assume pre-tax accounting profit ₹20 lakh, current tax expense ₹4 lakh and deferred tax expense ₹1 lakh. Cash tax paid is ₹3 lakh. Rates and amounts are hypothetical.~~Total tax expense = ₹4 + ₹1 = ₹5.;;Net profit = ₹20 − ₹5 = ₹15; effective tax rate = 25%.;;Current tax unpaid in this simplified example rises by ₹1; the deferred component is not an additional current cash payment.~~Accounting tax expense is ₹5 and cash tax payment is ₹3.~~A valuation must model tax cash flows consistently with operating profit, deductions and timing rather than substituting either figure mechanically.|Reconcile accounting and taxable profit using notes.;;Explain current, deferred and cash tax separately.;;Use jurisdiction- and period-specific primary rules before applying actual rates.
T05.16|Continuing operations represent the business remaining within the relevant reporting perimeter. A qualifying discontinued operation is separately presented under the applicable standard, allowing readers to distinguish ongoing performance from a major disposed or held-for-sale component. Not every closed product or sold asset meets that definition.~~Comparative information and notes help construct a like-for-like history. In valuation, forecast remaining operations and separately assess disposal proceeds, taxes, retained liabilities and transition costs. Removing discontinued earnings without considering associated assets, obligations and central costs can misstate ongoing economics.|Continuing operations: Activities included in the ongoing business after the relevant presentation assessment.;;Discontinued operations: Qualifying components separately presented under the applicable standard.;;Held-for-sale classification: A separate asset or disposal-group assessment that does not automatically mean discontinued operations.;;Stranded costs: Costs that remain after a business is sold and cannot immediately be removed.|An illustrative group earns ₹18 lakh from its remaining divisions and ₹5 from a qualifying discontinued division. It expects ₹40 cash disposal proceeds and ₹3 one-time transition costs. Ignore tax for this case.~~Separate the ₹18 ongoing earnings from ₹5 discontinued earnings.;;Forecast the remaining business rather than perpetuating total historical earnings of ₹23.;;Assess ₹40 proceeds, sale-related obligations and ₹3 transition costs separately.~~Ongoing earnings are ₹18 before considering future stranded-cost or synergy changes.~~A profitable sold division can reduce future earnings while increasing cash today. Disposal value and future operating profit require separate calculations.|Verify qualification and reporting-period treatment.;;Restate a consistent ongoing operating history.;;Model proceeds, transition costs, retained claims and stranded overhead.
T05.17|Net profit is the residual accounting earnings after relevant expenses. In consolidated accounts, the group can include 100% of controlled subsidiaries even where the parent owns less than 100%. Profit attribution then separates the parent owners' share from non-controlling interests. The parent-shareholder claim is not necessarily the whole consolidated profit.~~Attribution is an accounting allocation, not an immediate dividend payment. Parent-attributable profit can be retained in subsidiaries, restricted from remittance or reinvested. Equity valuation and EPS need consistent ownership boundaries, while consolidated operating valuation may require a non-controlling-interest adjustment in the equity bridge.|Consolidated net profit: Profit across the consolidated reporting perimeter after relevant expenses.;;Parent-attributable profit: The share attributable to owners of the parent.;;Non-controlling interests: Outside ownership claims within controlled subsidiaries.;;Retained profit and dividends: Accounting retention and cash distributions are distinct decisions.|A parent owns 80% of a subsidiary. The subsidiary earns ₹10 lakh after tax; the parent's other operations earn ₹7 lakh. Assume full consolidation and no other adjustments.~~Consolidated group profit = ₹10 + ₹7 = ₹17.;;Outside owners' attribution = 20% × ₹10 = ₹2.;;Parent-attributable profit = ₹17 − ₹2 = ₹15.~~The group reports ₹17 total profit but only ₹15 belongs to parent owners under these assumptions.~~A consolidated FCFF valuation includes the subsidiary's operations; the equity bridge must then recognise the outside owners' claim consistently.|Reconcile total profit with parent and NCI attribution.;;Distinguish ownership from control and cash remittance.;;Match valuation cash flows, claims and share counts to the same perimeter.
T05.18|Basic EPS divides earnings available to ordinary shareholders by weighted-average ordinary shares outstanding. Weighting matters because newly issued shares were not outstanding for the whole period. Diluted EPS incorporates qualifying dilutive potential ordinary shares under the applicable standard, with numerator and denominator adjustments where necessary.~~EPS growth can come from earnings growth or a smaller share count. Buybacks may raise EPS without improving operating performance, and financing costs can offset the benefit. Anti-dilutive instruments are not included simply because they exist; their treatment must follow the relevant EPS rules.|Basic EPS: Earnings for ordinary shareholders divided by time-weighted ordinary shares.;;Diluted EPS: A measure incorporating qualifying dilutive options, convertible instruments and other potential shares.;;Share-count effect: An EPS change caused by issuance or repurchase rather than operating earnings.;;Anti-dilutive instrument: An instrument excluded from diluted EPS when inclusion would improve EPS or reduce loss per share under the applicable rules.|Owners' earnings are ₹100 lakh. There are 40 lakh shares for six months and 60 lakh for six months. Assume no preference-share adjustment.~~Weighted shares = 40 × 0.5 + 60 × 0.5 = 50 lakh.;;Basic EPS = ₹100 lakh/50 lakh = ₹2.;;Using ending shares alone would give ₹1.67 and would ignore issuance timing.~~Correct basic EPS in this simplified case is ₹2.~~Dilution requires an additional instrument-specific calculation. A convertible bond cannot be assessed by adding shares while ignoring its eligible numerator adjustments.|Reconcile owners' earnings and weighted shares.;;Separate earnings effects from capital changes.;;Read the diluted-EPS reconciliation and instrument terms.
T05.19|Other comprehensive income contains specific income and expense items that the applicable framework recognises outside current profit. Total comprehensive income combines net profit and OCI, showing a wider set of recognised changes from non-owner activities. OCI is not simply a category for any item management dislikes.~~Different OCI items can follow different later treatment: some are reclassified to profit when conditions are met, while others are not. Foreign-operation translation, certain financial-asset changes and defined-benefit remeasurements illustrate why the note's nature matters. Both cash and non-cash items need careful interpretation.|Net profit: Items recognised in the profit-or-loss subtotal.;;OCI with reclassification: Items that may later move to profit under the governing rules.;;OCI without reclassification: Items whose subsequent treatment does not involve recycling to profit under those rules.;;Owner transactions: Capital contributions and distributions, separate from comprehensive income.|Opening equity is ₹100 lakh. Net profit is ₹16, OCI is a ₹3 loss, and dividends are ₹5. Assume no other equity changes.~~Comprehensive income = ₹16 − ₹3 = ₹13.;;Closing equity = ₹100 + ₹13 − ₹5 = ₹108.;;The dividend changes equity and cash but is not an expense inside comprehensive income.~~Equity rises ₹8 although net profit is ₹16.~~Examining only net profit misses the OCI loss and owner distribution. OCI also does not automatically equal a current cash movement.|Identify the nature and tax treatment of each OCI item.;;Check whether later reclassification is permitted or required.;;Reconcile comprehensive income and owner transactions to changes in equity.
T05.20|Segment reporting separates components whose performance is reviewed under the applicable reporting requirements. Segments can be business lines or other management-defined operating units; geographical disclosure serves an additional analytical purpose. Segment revenue or profit can use measures different from consolidated accounting subtotals and must be reconciled.~~Aggregate growth can hide deterioration in a major division or a shift toward low-margin products. Assess each segment's growth, margin, assets and capital needs, then consider shared costs and internal transactions. A profitable segment does not necessarily generate an attractive return after all required investment and central obligations.|Operating segment: A component identified under the applicable management and reporting criteria.;;Reportable segment: A segment presented separately when relevant criteria are met.;;Intersegment revenue: Internal activity requiring reconciliation so group totals do not double count it.;;Unallocated corporate cost: Shared costs outside segment measures that still affect group economics.|Division A has ₹100 lakh external revenue and ₹20 profit; Division B has ₹80 external revenue and ₹4 profit. Corporate costs are ₹6. Assume no intersegment sales or other adjustments.~~A margin = 20%; B margin = 5%.;;Combined segment profit = ₹20 + ₹4 = ₹24.;;Group profit after central costs = ₹24 − ₹6 = ₹18 on ₹180 revenue, a 10% margin.~~The group margin hides substantial differences between divisions.~~If B grows faster, group revenue can rise while overall margin declines. Capital allocation needs segment cash generation and returns, not just the headline group growth rate.|Reconcile segments to consolidated totals.;;Separate business mix from same-segment performance.;;Compare segment margins, capital employed, cash conversion and shared-cost allocation.
